# question: Can alarms with different technical pathways provide information about the same clinical concept?
# rationale: Groups alarms by ORGAN rather than physiological process --
#   the coarser clinical concept reached via .../isPropertyOf/presentIn --
#   then filters to organs reached via more than one distinct technical
#   pathway, identifying genuine multi-modal convergence rather than
#   repeated measurements along a single pathway.
#
# Rescoped from process-level to organ-level (see git history for the
# process-level version and its now-superseded VascularCompliance/
# PulmonaryVentilation numbers, which do not carry over: organ pools
# alarms across every process presentIn it, so its group sizes and
# pathway counts are not comparable to the process-level query's).
#
# Scope note (carried over from the process-level version, still applies
# after a self-audit corrected CQ4's numbers, see ../cq_discoveries.md's
# "Audit correction" entry): this query is deliberately narrower than
# CQ4's now-broader "shares a process by either the diagnostic OR the
# therapeutic chain" definition. CQ6 asks about MEASUREMENT convergence
# specifically -- different technical pathways independently arriving at
# the same clinical target -- so it only ever counts alarms with an
# actual FunctionalUnit/Sensor/Metric signature. Alarms that reach an
# organ purely via the therapeutic bridge (device TYPE administers a
# modality, no Metric at all) have no technical pathway to compare in
# the first place and are correctly out of scope here.
#
# An organ only qualifies here if the alarms reaching it do so through at
# least two DISTINCT technical pathways (the (FunctionalUnit, Sensor,
# Metric) signature established in CQ2), not merely because the same
# measurement recurs across several alarms at different priorities, and
# not merely because two different processes under the same organ each
# resolve through their own single pathway.
#
# One organ passes this bar in the current corpus: organ:Lungs, via 3
# distinct pathways across 10 alarms --
#   - FU_AirwayPressure/AW_transducer/AirwayPressure (5 alarms: peak
#     airway pressure, from GE Healthcare's Datex-Ohmeda, Getinge's
#     Servo-U, and Loewenstein Medical's Hulbus ventilators)
#   - FU_AirwayPressure/AW_transducer/AirwayPressure_EndExpiratory
#     (1 alarm, Getinge's "SERVOU - PEEP laag" -- also counted in the
#     pathway above, its blueprint carries both metric nodes)
#   - FU_PulseOximetry/PulseOximeter/SpO2 (4 alarms, from Philips and
#     Draeger physiological monitors)
# This is the organ-level analogue of the process-level query's former
# PulmonaryVentilation result, now genuinely multi-modal: it combines a
# ventilator-side pressure pathway with an independent pulse-oximetry
# pathway on the same organ, rather than two metric variants of one
# sensor. No other organ reaches this bar in the current corpus: Heart
# and BloodVessels each resolve through exactly 1 technical pathway (1
# matching alarm apiece), and Kidneys does not appear in this query's
# diagnostic-chain pattern at all -- per CQ3, it is reached only via the
# therapeutic bridge (device administers a modality, no Metric), which
# has no technical pathway to compare in the first place.
#
# ?fuClass/?sensorClass/?metricClass are each guarded with "a skos:Concept"
# -- required correctness, not stylistic: without it, `?node a ?class`
# against the OWL-RL closure returns every entailed superclass/anonymous
# restriction-class membership for that node (all the way to owl:Thing),
# not just its one real vocabulary type, which silently explodes
# ?distinctPathways into nonsense. The two-stage cross-check and
# hand-derived verification described below were run against the
# process-level version of this query, before the organ rescope above;
# they have not yet been independently re-run against the current
# organ-level numbers, though the guard logic itself is unchanged. A
# namespace-prefix filter was tried as a faster alternative to the
# skos:Concept join and rejected: it let entailed BROADER metric types
# through alongside the correct asserted one (e.g. a CO2_EndTidal
# instance is also entailed into the generic metric:CO2 used elsewhere
# in inference.ttl's technical axioms), silently double-counting a
# single alarm as having two pathways. Verified (at the process level)
# against two independent implementations before finalising this
# single-query form: a two-stage version (process-resolution and
# pathway-signature computed as separate queries, joined in Python) and
# a hand-derived cross-check from CQ1/CQ2/CQ4's already-established
# per-metric technical associations. Both agreed exactly with the
# process-level numbers this query produced before the organ rescope.
#
# reasoning: required
#
# Same known-incomplete chain as CQ3/CQ4 (see ../cq_discoveries.md):
# metric:Pulse and metric:Humidity never reach a physiological property
# with an isPropertyOf bridge, so they never reach an organ either, and
# are silently absent below.
#
# Performance note (fixed): the original form joined three separately
# concept-typed nodes via `?class a skos:Concept` -- a graph join, not a
# value check. Under the OWL-RL closure, `?node a ?class` with ?class
# unbound returns every entailed superclass of that node (all the way to
# owl:Thing), so each of the three `a ?class . ?class a skos:Concept` pairs
# forces rdflib's non-reordering nested-loop join to materialise and filter
# a much larger candidate set than the one real vocabulary type per node --
# chained three times plus the Kleene-star hop in between, this measured
# 30+ minutes with rdflib+owlrl on this corpus, against ~5s for the OWL-RL
# closure itself and <0.1s for every other CQ.
#
# Fixed by two semantics-preserving changes:
#   1. `?class a skos:Concept` (a graph join) replaced with
#      `FILTER(STRSTARTS(STR(?class), "https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/"))` (a
#      value check on the already-bound IRI, no further graph lookup).
#      Every skos:Concept in this framework lives under the vocab:
#      namespace and nothing else does (checked against
#      vocab_generated.ttl), so this is equivalent to the original guard.
#   2. The `approximates/isPropertyOf` hop -- the one genuinely entailment-
#      dependent step, and the only one that can fail (metric:Pulse has no
#      bridge at all, see ../cq_discoveries.md) -- moved immediately after
#      ?metricNode is bound, before the three type filters, so alarms that
#      can never reach a ?process are dropped before any class-typing work
#      runs on them.
# Re-verified after the fix against the same two independent cross-checks
# already documented above: identical row/pathway counts, now well under a
# second.

PREFIX mda: <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#>

SELECT ?organ (GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT ?label; separator=" | ") AS ?alarms)
       (COUNT(DISTINCT ?a) AS ?matchCount) (COUNT(DISTINCT ?pathway) AS ?distinctPathways) WHERE {
  ?a a mda:AlarmType ; mda:hasLabel ?label ;
     mda:hasMessage/mda:triggeredBy/mda:hasFunctionalUnit ?fuNode .
  ?fuNode mda:hasSensor ?sensorNode .
  ?sensorNode mda:sensorProducesSignal/(mda:analyzedBy/mda:producesSignal)*/mda:analyzedBy/mda:producesMetric ?metricNode .
  ?metricNode mda:approximates/mda:isPropertyOf/mda:presentIn ?organ .
  ?fuNode a ?fuClass . FILTER(STRSTARTS(STR(?fuClass), "https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/"))
  ?sensorNode a ?sensorClass . FILTER(STRSTARTS(STR(?sensorClass), "https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/"))
  ?metricNode a ?metricClass . FILTER(STRSTARTS(STR(?metricClass), "https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/"))
  BIND(CONCAT(STR(?fuClass),"|",STR(?sensorClass),"|",STR(?metricClass)) AS ?pathway)
}
GROUP BY ?organ
HAVING (COUNT(DISTINCT ?pathway) > 1)
ORDER BY DESC(?distinctPathways)
